# Machine-specific paths for this repo. Copy to `.env_vars` and edit.
#
#   cp .env_vars.example .env_vars
#
# `.env_vars` is GITIGNORED, and must stay that way: this repo is PUBLIC, and these are
# absolute paths on your machine. Note it is NOT called `.env` -- `.env` is already the
# general virtualenv DIRECTORY here (see `make env`), so a `.env` file would collide with it.
#
# Format is `VAR=value` (no `export`, no quotes needed), which is both make-includable
# (the Makefile `-include`s this file) and shell-sourceable:
#
#   set -a && . ./.env_vars && set +a
#
# Every value below is an EXAMPLE. Nothing here is a real path on anyone's machine -- fill in
# your own.

# Where the ORIGINAL track recordings live -- the `NNN-Artist - Title.ext` files that
# `streamalign track-mix` and the origNNN spans in `streamalign hints` correlate against
# the mix. The repo's `sources` symlink points at an external media volume that is often
# not mounted; point this at a copy that is always there.
#
# This is also where the harvester reads its Mystery Track queries from: a clip named
# `Mystery Track <N>.<wav|mp3|flac|m4a>` puts mystery N into the search. No clip, no search --
# see PROCESS.md, "Adding a new Mystery Track clip".
NETRADIO_SOURCES_DIR=/path/to/your/sources

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube cookies (OPTIONAL -- and OFF by default, deliberately)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube answers anonymous downloads with "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot." That error
# carries no 403 and no 429, so waiting does not clear it: only a signed-in session does. The
# harvester HALTS on it rather than grinding through the queue failing identically on every item.
#
# The cookie IS a credential -- it is your logged-in YouTube session. That is why the harvester
# never goes looking for one on its own, why these are unset by default, and why they live here
# in `.env_vars` (gitignored) and never in the repo.
#
# Set ONE of the two. A cookie FILE wins over a browser if both are set.
#
# 1. A cookies.txt file (Netscape format). PREFERRED on macOS -- it is just a file, so it needs
#    no Keychain, and it will not prompt you. See PROCESS.md, "Feeding the harvester a cookie".
#NETRADIO_YTDLP_COOKIES=/path/to/youtube-cookies.txt
#
# 2. Read them straight out of a browser profile: brave|chrome|chromium|edge|firefox|opera|safari
#    (anything yt-dlp accepts, including a named profile such as `chrome:Profile 2`).
#
#    WARNING, macOS: `chrome` (and any Chromium-based browser) stores its cookies ENCRYPTED and
#    decrypts them through the login Keychain, so every single invocation raises a
#    "security wants to use your ... Chrome Safe Storage" password prompt. A harvester that
#    restarts will prompt you again, and again. `firefox` does NOT have this problem -- it keeps
#    cookies in a plain `cookies.sqlite` -- so prefer firefox, or option 1 above.
#NETRADIO_YTDLP_COOKIES_FROM_BROWSER=firefox
